AI\n\nThis paper presents an analysis of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring 
of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. By 
revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, the paper aims to 
contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's significance in Minoan 
and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical scholarship and recent 
discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions related to the ring's 
motifs and their implications.\n\n## Key takeaways sparkles AI\n\n1. The text 
explores the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor' through iconographic 
analysis.\n2. Contradictions in technical analysis highlight the importance of 
iconography in Aegean studies.\n3. The connection between incurved altars and 
the ring's depiction supports its authenticity.\n4. The ring's iconography may 
reflect cultural practices from the 1920s.\n5. Generations of scholars have 
debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings.\n\n## Related 
papers\n\n[Towards a Consideration of Unprovenanced Ancient and Medieval Finger 
