\nTHE GRIFFIN WARRIOR TOMB is a Bronze Age shaft tomb dating to around 1450 BC. 
The gravesite was discovered in an olive grove near the ancient Palace of 
Nestor, located near the modern that city of Pylos, the most completely 
preserved of all Bronze Age palaces on the Greek mainland. The tomb is believed 
to belong to a warrior, possibly a figure reminiscent of those depicted in 
HOMER’S EPIC such as the ILIAD and THE ODYSSEY. Artifacts found in the tomb, 
including a bronze sword and a gold signet ring, suggest a high status, 
paralleling the heroic ideals celebrated in HOMERIC LITERATURE.   \n  \nThe 
discovery of the Griffin Warrior Tomb has prompted scholars to reconsider the 
historical accuracy of Homer’s works, suggesting that they may reflect REAL 
FIGURES AND EVENTS FROM THE MYCENAEAN PERIOD.   \n  \nNECKLACE SEEN HERE: “The 
chain of this beaded necklace was fashioned from 365 interlocking gold loops. 
Ivy-leaf motifs decorate the clasps, one of which was broken (in battle?) and 
repaired in antiquity. Elite Mycenaean and Minoan warriors wore jewelry, 
including armlets and bracelets strung with sealstones. Myriad carnelian, 
amethyst, amber, and gold beads were found in the Griffin Warrior’s burial, 
deposited by the living to adorn the hero’s body in death.”  \n  \nTHE GRIFFIN 
WARRIOR TOMB: The Griffin Warrior Tomb: During the initial six month excavation,
the research team uncovered an intact adult male skeleton and excavated 1400 
objects including weapons, jewels, armor and silver and gold artifacts. Since 
2015, the number of artifacts recovered from the grave has reached over 3500 
items, including a historically significant Minoan sealstone called the Pylos 
Combat Agate and four signet gold rings with detailed images from Minoan 
