    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This conference paper on Aegean Bronze Age 
archaeology introduces the concept of gemmae dubitandae—doubtful sealstones and 
signet rings—using the 'Ring of Nestor' as a key example. It outlines the 
historiography of scholarly debate about the ring’s authenticity. The text notes
that Martin Nilsson initially treated the ring as an authentic Bronze Age 
artifact in the first edition of his book on Minoan-Mycenaean religion, but in 
the 1950 second edition he reclassified it as a suspect object in response to 
growing concerns about fakes. Subsequently, Hagen Biesantz (1954) argued against
the ring’s authenticity based mainly on stylistic anomalies. Agni 
Xenaki-Sakellariou (1994) discussed iconographic details she considered 
suspicious and criticized the ring’s typology and technical features. Ingo 
Pini’s 1998 article, presented as a response to Xenaki-Sakellariou, rebutted her
arguments and brought forward new evidence in support of authenticity, drawing 
on more recent comparative material. Overall, the document uses the Ring of 
Nestor to illustrate how scholarly views on authenticity shift over time and how
such suspect objects are positioned within relational archaeology.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "This paper analyzes the iconography of the 'Ring of 
Nestor' within the broader issues of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. 
It examines specific motifs on the ring, such as incurved altars, comparing them
with other Aegean Bronze Age material to assess whether they support or 
undermine the ring’s authenticity. The author emphasizes that technical analyses
of the object have produced contradictory results, making iconographic study 
especially important. The text notes that generations of scholars have disputed 
the genuineness of the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, and argues against 
dismissing unprovenanced finger rings outright, suggesting they can still 
contribute to understanding Minoan and Mycenaean art and ritual if handled 
critically. One conclusion highlighted in the key points is that the association
between incurved altars and the ring’s imagery can be interpreted as supporting 
its authenticity, though the paper also entertains the possibility that some 
aspects of the iconography could reflect cultural practices from the early 20th 
century, complicating the issue.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "This article reports the discovery of an unlooted 
Bronze Age warrior’s tomb at Pylos in southwestern Greece, near the Mycenaean 
Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos. An international team led by University of 
Cincinnati archaeologists uncovered the grave, which dates to around 1500 BC and
contained more than 1,400 artifacts including jewels, weapons, armor, and 
precious metal vessels. The article highlights several key finds: a gold ring 
decorated with a Cretan bull‑jumping scene, identified as one of four solid‑gold
rings from the tomb; multiple ivory combs; a long gold necklace with pendants; 
over four dozen seal stones with intricate Minoan designs, often featuring 
long‑horned bulls and human bull‑jumpers; and a bronze mirror with an ivory 
handle. The text stresses that the number of solid gold rings in a single burial
is greater than for any other known Greek burial and emphasizes the rich, intact
nature of the tomb and its proximity to Nestor’s palace.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "This Smithsonian Magazine article describes the 
discovery in May 2015 of an intact Mycenaean warrior’s grave near the Palace of 
Nestor at Pylos, excavated by Shari Stocker and Jack Davis of the University of 
Cincinnati. The warrior, dubbed the Griffin Warrior, was a man in his mid‑30s 
who died around 1500 BC and was buried with roughly 2,000 objects including 
silver cups, precious stone beads, ivory combs, a sword, and four solid gold 
rings with intricate decoration. The article situates these finds in the broader
relationship between the Minoan civilization on Crete and the mainland Mycenaean
culture, proposing that the grave demonstrates close connections rather than 
simple conquest. The rings are described as bearing Minoan imagery, including 
the sacred bull motif on two rings, and the grave also contained a bronze bull’s
head staff. Expert Cynthia Shelmerdine is quoted interpreting the Minoan rings 
and other objects as markers of political power and evidence of increasingly 
close ties between mainland elites and elites on Crete, irrespective of whether 
the rings were used in traditional Minoan sealing practices.",
